      <Text>dresser, yeah. I mean it also is like, I think it's really clear from Imelda is whatever, from the way Imelda tells the story, even though she doesn't tell it this way, you can you just know that Imelda picked him and built his career, that she was like, I am going to get to the top through this, man, yeah, and I'm gonna do everything it takes to kind of stage and dramaturg this performance. And then, you know the title of the play, Here Lies Love. Is what she put on his headstone. It's his gravestone. And she tells this romantic tale of how, this is my great love lost, and so that's why I put on his headstone. Here Lies Love. There is my love. So it's like it defeats imelda's own performance of the great love story, historically, you know, please. But like anyway, that's imelda's Here Lies Love. But the play Here Lies Love doesn't even allude to her narrative of this incredible lifelong romance, or something.</Text>
