      <Text>SPEAKER 3</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>That's our stage not a stage manager, but our stage directions reader. We called her the assistant director. Her mom is a famous on Philippine de. Her mom is a famous Filipino, American producer. Got it, okay?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SPEAKER 2</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Yeah. So on opening night, a statement was</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>UNKNOWN SPEAKER</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>read on opening night. On brought from,</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SPEAKER 2</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>no, here, oh, here I went to opening night. Oh, and she was Alex's mom was in the Philippines, and she's like, we have, we have a statement from the executive producer, or whatever her title was, from G she's currently in the Philippines, but celebrating her dad's birthday, but she wanted to read this, and it was essentially like, welcome. We the reason that we did this play was because there are the LA is the biggest Filipino diaspora in the nation. And we hope that this piece is a conversation starter. It is at no means meant to glorify the happenings, but it's a conversation starter. Wow, of how we got there. And I hope you enjoy and continue talking about this show. It was like they're apologizing for the show. She was like, apologizing for the show before it even,</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>UNKNOWN SPEAKER</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>because she knew the context in LA,</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SPEAKER 2</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>like, you're not gonna like this, but think of it as like, well, this is how we talk about it. Yeah. So that's where the problem lies. It's like,</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>UNKNOWN SPEAKER</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Here lies the problem.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SPEAKER 4</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>And like in the whole thing is like in, like, Les Miserables, they don't even mention the people that are being oppressed, right? Let alone them giving, yeah, the oppressors aren't even mentioned, like, let alone getting music numbers, let the main character the whole story, right, right? You know, like, I don't want to see how, like Louis the whatever, had a horrible childhood.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SPEAKER 1</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Yeah, you know, well, Abigail was talking about Sophia Coppola's depiction of Marie Antoinette as a possible analog, where it was all like soft lens, and what cakes can I eat, and what dresses am I wearing? And about, like, the scene and not about, yeah, you're gonna get your head shot. Yeah, yeah,</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>UNKNOWN SPEAKER</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I guess I do leave.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SPEAKER 3</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I mean, I don't. I also don't believe in glorifying Marie Antoinette. But, like,</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>UNKNOWN SPEAKER</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>feel as</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SPEAKER 3</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>there's something about dear life's love that really asks the audience to be a fan of Imelda Marcos for quite a lot of the play, yeah. And also the audience loved it and ate it all up.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SPEAKER 2</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>So what was interesting, yeah, can I say, well, being there on opening night versus being there on a later date?</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>UNKNOWN SPEAKER</Text>
