    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>UNKNOWN SPEAKER</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>We saw it. We booed and like, people like, looked at us, yeah, yeah. And we're like, Oh, you don't know,</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SPEAKER 3</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>and like so for almost all, I mean, for the whole play, I never applauded Imelda or Marcos or the bad government, but I applaud for, like Pinoy Kino songs and like the revolution songs, even though they were the worst songs and clearly written after the fact. But like, I everybody else loved all those songs, and it was tons of Filipinos in the audience. Like, people were coming with, like, you know, like, like eight people from their family, yeah? Or, like, their three best girlfriends, or whatever. Like, there were all kinds of selfies being done beforehand. And then everybody, like, moves in together. Merch was being sold, yeah. And then, like, everybody's like, yay. The whole time, like, hooray, yay. Good, good number. I like the dancing, like everybody in the whole theater was and then it ended, and then everybody gave standing ovations. And the only time there was any sentiment of, like, a political, like a political sentiment in the audience was at the very, very end, the last thing projected is that Imelda son is now present in the Philippines. And then people were like, oh, like, was like a surprise, yeah.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SPEAKER 2</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>It was like, definitely behind me. The first time I saw it. She that last, that last projection, yeah, she goes the fuck yeah.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SPEAKER 3</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Blackout, so, I mean, partly, this is about, you know, like, I have a there was somebody who is a close, like one of the my closest students, when I was a grad student and she was an undergrad, was a Filipina American in at Northwestern, and she now lives in happily Bay, and she and I have talked, over the years many times about her fandom for Imelda. And like, the last time we talked about this, which was, like, recently, I was like, Kat, you got to drop the Imelda fan. Yeah. Like, it's not okay. And especially now you can see the Trump gilded Ivanka Melania thing. You have to see how bad it is to be a fan of Imelda. And she said, Well, you know, growing up, there just wasn't any other Filipino person to be a fan of, and Imelda is like this glamorous whole hand. And I really think many Filipino and Filipino American people only relate to Filipino culture and history in these very surface ways, right? Where it's like, not real history. It's just like, well, this person's famous and this person's famous and this person's famous, and one of them is a fucking dictator, but that they're all just celebrities, yeah, you know, because I was</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SPEAKER 4</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>so confused that, why like, like, in the musical, The Wedding Singer, there always has to be a Filipino woman in the cast, because there's a character that's an Imelda, like impersonation. And I'm like, Why the hell is this character? Yeah, then that also means that you can't cast the Philips, like, if you have no more than one Filipino person, that's the only part they can. That's interesting, which drives me crazy. Yeah, it's like, well, or</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>UNKNOWN SPEAKER</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Yeah, but you're saying in the 80s,</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SPEAKER 2</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>she her status. That's why wedding singers, like a wedding singer, wasn't</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>UNKNOWN SPEAKER</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
