    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SPEAKER 1</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>I mean, now you know, they're in my performing diversity class, which was about diversity and all the things, including theater, one of my Latina students, did a little presentation on like, West Side Story, and she used our labs, tagging methodology, and she was like, you know, at the end she she gave it a score. I don't remember if it was like, good or bad. She gave she wrote the blur. And the whole thing was very confused, because it was like, there were lots of positive and lots of negatives and everything just kind of seemed to average out. And at the end, I was like, Well, what do you really think of West Side Story, though? I mean, do you hate it? Is it just boring? Is it mediocre? Like, from all everything you did with this, I can't even really tell where you landed. And she was like, that's because I hate it. And so does everybody, like all Latinos hate it, and we hope it gets produced every season everywhere, and we audition every time, all of us, and we go out really hard for the audition, and we don't care when a West Side Story opens. We're like, great. Now we're all going to audition for that. And I'm like, that's that's sad, but now we have ours.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>UNKNOWN SPEAKER</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>We're getting closer to equality.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SPEAKER 1</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>Welcome to neoliberal America. Every minority gets a shitty, shitty musical that makes a lot of money that every actor from that group wants to audition for every time. But, like,</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SPEAKER 2</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>the thing is, though, is that, I mean, some people do love the stone, some people like but, like, with Miss Saigon is the same thing where it's like, yeah, I know a lot of people that are like, I would love to do something else, anything guys commit suicide for a white man. Yeah, anything else but that? Yeah, I know. And that means so that got every Filipino woman their</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SPEAKER 1</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>equity card I know, which I sometimes wonder about Vietnamese American actors, and what they think about Miss Saigon being like a showcase for Filipino Americans. But one time I was, I was in England, you know, I did my one semester abroad in England, and it was so horrible, and there were no Filipino people, anywhere the food, except for South Asian food, is tragic. And I said, I am going to go see some Filipino people right now. And I took the train to London and bought a ticket to masaga. I was like, I'm just going to be in the company of Filipino people. And John, John was the engineer, wow, production. So that was great. But like, it was weird, because you're like, this actually happened in the Philippines too. Like, it could just be about Philippines, but instead it's about Vietnamese women. But that's okay, I guess. Is it okay? I'm not sure, but yeah, I see white men</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SPEAKER 2</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>because it's yeah, of course, that's where it all comes back to, yeah.</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SPEAKER 1</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>All right, yeah. I mean, all of these shows we're talking about are strange. They're very odd, and it's odd how much money they make, and people love them, and like, she kills herself, and then was Saigon, and people like, I'm crying, I'm like, What is even, what are you doing? Should be angry. You know, the whole audience all the time in these shows, how beautiful. I told</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Character">
      <Text>SPEAKER 2</Text>
    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>you about the play. Not another truth, not this is not a this is,</Text>
    </Paragraph>
