    </Paragraph>
    <Paragraph Type="Dialogue">
      <Text>learn, you learn later that the reason why we keep seeing it over and over again is that it's a metaphor for how many times that play gets produced. So every time there we see their character die, like, oh, another production is happening. Oh, another protection is happening. And it gets to this thing where some they're like, We need to stop this. Yeah. Like, we we can tell other stories. We can tell our own story. But every time they do, every time they do, they get it gets reset and, yeah, relive it over and over again. So it's about like trying to reclaim your story and what possibly that looks like, yeah, and in this version, it like it ends up like destroying the book that they're attracted, yeah, and they the in the stage directions. It's like, the page is rip. Every time they go off script, a rip, a rip in the script happens until at the end where they they just fully rip up the script. Never performs again. And they're like, What do we do? And they're like, I guess we write our own story. So it ends with them writing their own Okay,</Text>
    </Paragraph>
